// lowerthird-demo — a broadcast-style caption for compositing over video: a translucent
// black panel across the foot of the frame, the rest fully transparent, holding a large,
// clearly readable scripture verse. It uses only engine primitives — no document package.
// The three pieces are \pagecolor{transparent} (an unpainted page, so everything outside
// the panel shows the video through it), \colorbox[alpha] (the translucent fill), and a
// \parbox (the panel, sized to its text plus \fboxsep padding).
//
// Render as a transparent PNG, one pixel per point, so every length below is in pixels:
//   texish -t png -r 72 scripts/lowerthird-demo.script
// The frame is 1280x720 (720p); for 1080p use 1920x1080 and scale the type up to match.

// A half-inch (36px at 72 dpi) margin on the top, left and right; the panel sits flush to
// the bottom edge, so the text area is inset on three sides and open at the foot.
\geometry paperwidth:1280pt paperheight:720pt top:0.5in left:0.5in right:0.5in bottom:0
\pagecolor{transparent}

// Push the panel to the foot of the frame.
\vfill

// The panel fills the width of the text area (1208px) — its \parbox is 1152px and \fboxsep
// adds 28px of padding on each side, so 1152 + 2*28 = 1208. It grows to fit whatever text
// it holds. The verse is set large in EB Garamond so it stays legible over moving footage.
\set fboxsep {28}
\colorbox[0.6]{black}{\parbox{1152pt}{
\color{white}
\font ebgaramond 46 regular
For God so loved the world that He gave His one and only Son, that everyone who believes
in Him shall not perish but have eternal life.
\par
\vskip 16
\font ebgaramond 30 bold
— John 3:16
}}
